  <content>
  <section><title>Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions
</title>
<para>You just ordered the steps for solving a system of equations using elimination. Try
  using these steps when the system has equations containing fractions.</para>
<para>When the system of equations contains fractions, we will first clear the fractions by
  multiplying each equation by the least common denominator (LCD) of all the
  fractions in the equation.</para>
<para>Solve the system by elimination: \( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} x + \frac{1}{2}y =6\\ \frac{3}{2}x + \frac{2}{3}y = \frac{17}{2} \end{array}\right. \)</para>

<!--BEGIN STEPS-->
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).
  Both equations are in standard form.
  \(x+\frac12 y=6\)
  \(\frac32x + \frac23 y=\frac{17}{2} \) 
 <emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).
  \(2(x+\frac12 y=6)\)
  \(6(\frac32 x+\frac23 y=\frac{17}{2})\)
  <emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify the equations.
   \(2x+y=12\)
  \(9x+4y=51\)
 <emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Eliminate one of the variables and simplify the equation.	
  \(-4(2x+y=12)=- 8x-4y=-48\)
 <emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Add the two equations to eliminate \(y\). 
  <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->
  \(\begin{align*}-8x-4y&amp;=-48
  \\9x+4y&amp;=51
  \\ \hline
   x&amp;=3
  \end{align*}\)
<!--End ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
<emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Solve for \(y\) by substituting \(x\) back into one of the equations.	
<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
\(\begin{align*}2x+y&amp;=12
\\2(3)+y&amp;=12
\\6+y&amp;=12
\\y&amp;=6
\\\end{align*}\)
<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
<emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Write the solution as an ordered pair.	
The ordered pair is \((3,6)\).	
<emphasis>Step 8 -</emphasis> Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.	
 <!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
</para><para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
    &amp; \frac32 x+\frac23 y= \frac{17}{2}	\\
  x+\frac12 y \overset?=6 &amp; \frac32 (3)+\frac23 (6) \overset?= \frac{17}{2}\\
  3+\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac92 +4 \overset?=\frac{17}{2}	\\
  3+3\overset?=6 &amp; \frac92 + \frac82 \overset?=\frac{17}{2} \\  
  6=6\checkmark &amp; \frac{17}{2}=\frac{17}{2}\checkmark
  \end{array}\)</para>
  <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
 
 <para>The solution is \((3,6)\).</para>
<!-- END STEPS -->
<note class="try look-at-me"/>
<title>Try It: Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions</title>
<para>Solve the following system of equations.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}\frac13\;x\;-\;\frac12y\;=\;1\\\frac34x\;\;-\;y\;=\;\frac52\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
   <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).
    Both equations are in standard form.
    \(\frac13x-\frac12y=1\\\frac34x-y=\frac52\)
   <emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).
     \({\style{color:red}6}(\frac13x-\frac12y) ={\style{color:red}6}(1)\\{\style{color:red}4}(\frac34x-y)={\style{color:red}4}(\frac52)\)
    <emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify the equations.
    \(2x-3y=6\)
    \(3x-4y=10\)
   <emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> To get equivalent coefficients of \(x\), we will need to multiply the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 2.	
    \({\style{color:red}3} (2 x – 3 y ) = {\style{color:red}3} ( 6 ) \)
       \({\style{color:red}2} (3 x – 4 y ) = {\style{color:red}2} ( 10 )\) 
   <emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Simplify. 	
    \(6 x – 9 y = 18\)
    \(6 x – 8 y = 20\) 
   <emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Subtract the equations to eliminate one variable. 
    <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->		
    \(\begin{align*}6x-9y&amp;=18
    \\6x-8y&amp;=20
    \\ \hline
    -y&amp;=-2
    \\y&amp;=2
    \end{align*}\)
    <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
   <emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Solve for \(x\) by substituting \(y\) back into one of the equations.	
    <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->	
    \(\begin{align*}2x-3y&amp;=6
    \\2x-3(2)&amp;=6
    \\2x-6&amp;=6
    \\2x&amp;=12
    \\x&amp;=6
    \\\end{align*}\)
    <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
   <emphasis>Step 8 -</emphasis> Write the solution as an ordered pair.	
    The ordered pair is \((6,2)\).	
   <emphasis>Step 9 -</emphasis> Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.	</para>
      
      <!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
    
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
      &amp; \frac34 -y = \frac{5}{2}	\\
        &amp; \frac34 (6)-2= \frac52	\\
      \frac13 x-\frac12 \overset?=1 &amp; \frac{18}{4}-2 \overset?= \frac52 \\
      \frac13 (6)\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac{18}{4} -\frac84 \overset?=\frac52	\\
      2-1\overset?=1 &amp; \frac92 + \frac{10}{4} \overset?= \frac52 \\  
      1=1\checkmark &amp; \frac52=\frac52 \checkmark
      \end{array}\)</para>
      
      <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
  
  <para>
  The solution is \((6,2)\).</para>
  
  <!-- END STEPS -->

  

  
  

  
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
